# SRG-APP-000121-AU-000130  -  CAT II
# The Central Log Server must protect audit tools from unauthorized access.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations, then kept
# by hand. Edit this file to change how this requirement is assessed here;
# it is read at run time. Regenerating refreshes the benchmark text in place
# and leaves the criteria, the determination and any verification block as
# they are.

id:              SRG-APP-000121-AU-000130
group:           V-221917
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      derived
revised:         yes
cci:             CCI-001493
control_rev5:    AU-9

contribution:    Splunk's audit tools are its own search, reporting and configuration
                 endpoints, and the application's share is to keep the capability that
                 bypasses every access control off the general-purpose roles.

governs:         authorize.conf - Used to create roles.

check:           authorize.conf [role_user] admin_all_objects absent
check:           authorize.conf [role_power] admin_all_objects absent

external:        Ownership and mode of the Splunk binaries and installation directories -
                 SPLK-CL-000190 implements this with splunk-user ownership and mode 700 at
                 the operating system layer
external:        Object-level access control lists recorded in default.meta, which the
                 btool-based collector does not read and which must be reviewed by hand

citation:        Both original conditions produced false findings. restmap.conf [<endpoint
                 stanza>] capability present fails any deployment that adds no REST
                 endpoints of its own: the stanza is the declaration of an additional
                 endpoint an app brings (restmap.conf.spec:70, 303-312), while Splunk's own
                 endpoints are gated by capabilities compiled into the product, so a
                 conformant install has no such stanza to read. authorize.conf
                 list_all_objects absent is both vacuous and inverted - the shipped
                 authorize.conf grants it in the [default] stanza (line 193), which applies
                 to every role, so no role stanza carries it and the condition always
                 passes while every role in fact holds it; and spec:458-460 describes that
                 capability as the control that "prevents unauthorized access to
                 configuration endpoints", so demanding its absence works against its
                 purpose. admin_all_objects is the capability the specification itself
                 calls an ACL bypass and it ships on role_admin alone, which makes it the
                 one condition here that passes a stock install and fails a real weakening.
finding:         A general-purpose role holds admin_all_objects, so the access control list
                 on every saved search, report and configuration object used as an audit
                 tool is bypassed for accounts that are not privileged.
